WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:11.780
Good evening, guys. This is a video about studying 
multiple foreign languages and...

00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:17.220
how you can switch between those different languages 
without getting confused and mixed up

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.180
If you looked at my channel before, you've seen that
I've studied a number of different foreign languages.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.480
Maybe you've studied a number of different foreign languages 
and maybe you found it kind of confusing or...

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:33.140
maybe you've found there's some overlap between the language 
or you've crossed wires between language.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.320
Maybe it gets confusing when you study more than one.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.260
how do you keep them all inside your head 
and be able to speak

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:44.300
more than a couple languages like five, 
six, seven languages. How do you do it?

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.300
Well, keep in mind I'm talking from the point of view 
of someone who's studied all these foreign languages
as an adult.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.360
I didn't learn any foreign language or
second language as a kid

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:00.400
I was raised monolingual, speak English. So this
is from that point of view of learning as an adult.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:03.720
Someone who learned it as a kid might be different.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.080
Just keep that in mind. Ok so basically...

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.540
There are three things. I will start with the first one:

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:15.100
I think you really need to refine 
your pronunciation for each of the languages.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:20.800
You really need to get specific ... 
with the distinctions you make in the phonology.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.540
So really look at all the different sounds 
in each language and get them as...

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:28.060
as specific and as precise as possible.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.640
Because  I think  a lot of what causes crossover 
between  different languages is that...

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:39.020
you are using the same phonology 
or similar phonology for those languages.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.160
When a native speaker has a very precise
distinct phonology for that language.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.020
So this is something ...[/]   My theory on this 
came from studying a few different languages.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:58.180
I studied Hebrew and Arabic which are quite related 
but the phonology isn't that similar, they're quite different.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:05.460
But also I've studied French and I find that I've had 
a lot more crossover between French and Hebrew

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:10.720
than between Arabic and Hebrew, even though 
French and Hebrew are not related really.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:20.620
And that's because there are some similar
phonological sounds between Hebrew and French 
don't exist between Hebrew and Arabic.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:26.620
So that's caused some of the crossover between them. 
It's the phonology, it's the similar phonology.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.560
So in order to solve that problem, I had to
really get precise with my phonology

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.980
for French and for Hebrew. 
I had to look at all the sounds, look at...

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:39.200
exactly how to pronounce those sounds
that were causing the confusion.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:45.240
So you can do that, I mean... when I say 
refine your pronunciation, it's in general...

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.020
focus on studying your pronunciation and improving it.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:52.220
Get some help from native speakers, 
really focus on emulating native speakers.

00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.580
But also at a more advanced level, 
I'll teach you a little technique that ...

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.300
that helps people take it to a more 
native level or near-native level.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:06.120
The technique is this : imagine that...

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:10.220
a particular native speaker 
is actually speaking through you.

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:16.260
It might sound strange but they're your kind of 
channeling a certain person through your body 
and they're speaking out your mouth.

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:23.240
Like when I speak French, I imagine that Sarkozy, 
the former president, is speaking through me.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:29.480
Because he's somebody that I modeled when I was 
studying French, I have paid attention to
his pronunciation, tried to copy his intonation.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.320
So when I speak French,

00:03:32.660 --> 00:03:37.420
when I'm trying to get precise with my pronunciation, 
I imagine it's him speaking through me.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:40.600
Right? And when I speak tagalog, 
I imagine a certain...

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:46.000
filipino person who I know, who speaks tagalog.
I Imagine that person is speaking through me.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.840
And by doing that, you're really...

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:56.520
Your brain is picking up on a lot of very little subtle cues 
in the pronunciation that you can't pick up on consciously.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.500
if you try to focus on all those little differences,
you can't really do it but...

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:10.160
if you just focus on the whole person, your unconscious 
mind can pick up on all those little fine-tuned elements
of the pronunciation and it helps you out a lot.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:16.860
So that's one thing, one trick you can try for getting 
your pronunciation to a more authentic level.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.140
Okay so focus on the pronunciation :
 that's my first piece of advice.

00:04:21.140 --> 00:04:25.140
The second thing is 100% commitment.

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:30.580
No half stepping 100% commitment 
when you are switching into the next language.

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:39.140
Okay, so when you are moving from one language 
into the other, it's kind of like you are walking through 
a doorway into another room.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.460
If you enter the doorway 
and you just stand in the doorway,

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:46.120
and try to speak the other language of the other room,

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.140
you're going to retreat into the first room,
you're going to go back into the other language.

00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:56.480
Or you're going to get some crossover between the 2, 
you'll find it hard to really move into the 2nd language.

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:01.900
So what you really need to do is just make a firm decision. 
Okay, now, I'm switching into the other language.

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:08.120
I'm not going back into the old one, I'm walking through 
the doorway and I'm closing the door behind me.

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:09.360
Right?So, you're...

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:13.920
You're basically just completely deciding:
I'm speaking this language now and not the other.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:17.520
You don't want to switch back and forth. 
So you want to just lock the door behind you.

00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:22.560
Okay so... it's really just about pushing yourself 
and making a firm commitment. And...

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:28.040
When you make that firm decision to speak this one,
then it motivates you and pushes you forward.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.640
And it takes you into that zone of that language. Ok?

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.380
So that's the second thing, just that 100% decision.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:38.780
Okay I'm going to speak French now, 
I'm going to speak arabic now.

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.420
And completely cut off the language 
you were speaking a moment earlier.

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:44.260
Alright. So that's number two.

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:50.160
Number three is a little more abstract and it's one 
that I don't use all that often anymore, but I used it.

00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.960
I've tried it in the past and made use of it
when I needed it. And it's like this :

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.680
You imagine a scene that you associate
with the language that you want to speak.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.820
So, for example, when I speak French, 
I might imagine a scene,

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.020
on the patio of a café in Paris. Right?

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:14.920
And imagine all of the the subtle little sensory cues 
that go along with that, like...

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.900
the taste of wine and the smell of my baguette.

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.280
And all that kind of stuff and hearing the ambience around 
me, hearing the French language spoken around me.

00:06:24.920 --> 00:06:26.540
So by doing that, you're...

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.440
creating a connection between 
the language and that image.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:32.820
So that, whenever you want to move into that...

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.600
that state in which is you are speaking the language well,

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.640
then you imagine that image and 
it brings you into the zone of that language.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:43.640
Okay. So that's something you can try.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:47.420
Another example of it when I speak Hebrew, I imagine...

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.500
the time I spent in Jerusalem 
because that's a very vivid time for me.

00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:58.340
So I can bring myself back to that time, imagine the
environment, imagine a certain situation I was in, in that place.

00:06:58.460 --> 00:07:03.320
Where I can hear Hebrew speakers 
and I see different cultural things,

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:05.440
that remind me of the Hebrew language and all of that.

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.980
So all of those things are associations that bring me 
back into the zone when I was speaking Hebrew well.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.740
All of these pieces of advice are kind of abstract.
I wish I could be a bit more...

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.120
logical and scientific in the tips I'm giving.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:20.860
Well, when we're dealing with...

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:26.580
We're dealing with switching from one language
to another, we're dealing with switching in two different states.

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:30.960
And that kind of requires you to take control of your perception.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:33.680
Right. It's not the same for everybody so if...

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:38.080
these tips don't really work for you 
then you don't have to use them of course, just...

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:43.860
but think about similar types of techniques 
and how you can control your perception in a way that...

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:48.160
will take you into that zone where you are
speaking the language well.

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.660
So a lot of it has to do with associations 
and connections with that language.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:55.320
Another thing I should point out is that...

00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:00.460
the languages that you haven't spoken recently 
are always going to fade a little bit...

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.480
if you leave them in the background right?

00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:07.660
The ones that you can move into the most easily
are the ones you have used most recently.

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:11.980
So don't expect to use one of these techniques and ...

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:17.840
be able to speak really well and really really instantly 
in a language you haven't spoken in five years.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:21.360
You might be able to with the basics of that language.

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:24.560
but you won't be at the level you were
at it, five years ago, obviously.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.160
Because you start to lose 
some of the language gradually.

00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:31.120
So just keep that in mind.
It's also a matter of...

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.420
just keeping the language fresh and keeping them...

00:08:33.620 --> 00:08:36.640
well practiced. You can't always do that with all of them.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:40.000
If you speak 10 languages, 
you can't speak all 10 everyday.

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:42.500
So you'll always have some that are more...

00:08:42.500 --> 00:08:46.860
ready to go than others and the others
are more waiting in the background for you to ...

00:08:46.860 --> 00:08:50.220
retrieve them and start to practice and...

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:52.600
and brush up on those languages.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:57.440
And then, they'll be in the forefront 
and ready to get into the zone again.

00:08:57.440 --> 00:09:01.240
Alright... so that's all you got to say about that.
I hope that's clear.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:02.260
'talk to you later...

